      "Dear colleagues, If only we had asked residents about the service before designing its interface. We were convinced that a mobile application would make the registration process easier. Instead, we reproduced the most confusing parts of the paper form and made them harder to discuss with a human being. Several people gave up halfway through, but our dashboard counted only those who reached the final screen.",
      "I would rather we acknowledged that the project was shaped by an untested assumption than present the difficulties as a failure of users to adapt. It is time we stopped describing accessibility as an optional improvement to be added after launch. The support team had warned us that some applicants shared devices or relied on intermittent connections. We heard the warning; we did not act on it.",
      "The revised service will offer assisted registration alongside the digital form, and the next evaluation will measure incomplete applications rather than successful submissions alone. Those changes do not undo the inconvenience we caused. They may, however, make this letter more than a ritual expression of regret. Accountability begins when an explanation changes a decision.",
      "We are now inviting people who abandoned the old service to test the revised process. Their participation will be paid, and declining the invitation will not affect their applications. Those conditions were missing from our first research plan, when we assumed that enthusiastic volunteers adequately represented all users. I hope the new process helps, but I no longer regard a tidy success metric as a substitute for listening to those who are hardest to reach."
