      "These provisions are not intended to guarantee agreement. Their purpose is to make disagreement visible and procedurally fair. The panel will publish not only its recommendation but also a record of the evidence it relied upon, the alternatives considered and the reasons certain objections were not adopted. A transparent decision is not necessarily the decision everyone wanted; it is one whose reasoning can be examined.",
      "The final report will distinguish the evidence the panel accepts from the values it prioritises. Two members may agree that a policy reduces average waiting times while disagreeing about whether that benefit justifies a burden placed on a vulnerable group. Recording the difference is not an admission of incompetence. It allows future reviewers to see where a judgement was made, rather than mistaking that judgement for a technical fact."
